<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- THE ROOT CAUSE of "selecting Flutter Autofill Service in phone Settings
     crashes the app, and it won't even open afterward until switched back
     to Google":

     This file has no <meta-data> reference anywhere in our own
     AndroidManifest.xml — it's picked up purely because it has the exact
     same resource name (res/xml/autofill_service_config.xml) as the file
     the flutter_autofill_service plugin's own library manifest points its
     <service android:name="android.autofill" .../> meta-data at. Gradle's
     resource merger lets an app-module resource silently replace a
     library-module resource of the same type+name, so whatever this file
     says becomes the *entire* live config for the OS-registered autofill
     service — with no visible wiring in our own manifest to warn future
     readers that it's even in play.

     It previously said android:settingsActivity="com.hanif.mymanager.MainActivity".
     Per Android's own autofill-services docs, settingsActivity is OPTIONAL,
     and — critically — on several OEM Settings UIs (MIUI/Xiaomi, Vivo,
     ColorOS/Oppo, Realme: exactly the brands this app's battery-exemption
     prompt already has to work around elsewhere) picking a service in
     system Settings does NOT just bind it quietly; the OS immediately
     launches whatever Activity is named here, via an explicit Intent
     started from the Settings app/task — not a normal launcher tap.

     We were pointing that straight at MainActivity: a singleTask,
     exported, multi-intent-filter Activity that on a normal cold start
     boots the full Flutter engine and main() (WorkManager registration,
     overlay-bubble restore, notification init, share-intent listeners...).
     Launched instead from that abnormal Settings-triggered path, this can
     throw where a normal launch wouldn't, and because MainActivity is
     singleTask, the broken instance is what a later tap on the home-screen
     icon resumes — so the app stays unable to open until the autofill
     service is switched away, which is exactly the symptom reported.

     Fix: declare no settingsActivity at all. Nothing about this app
     requires a system-triggered setup screen — enabling and configuring
     autofill is already done from inside the app's own Settings tab
     (AutofillService().requestSetAutofillService(), see settings_screen.dart)
     — so there's nothing to lose by leaving the OS with no Activity to
     auto-launch here, and doing so removes this crash trigger entirely. -->
<autofill-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android" />
